: "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_ring_count",\\n      "description": "Return total ring count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_aromatic_rings",\\n      "description": "Return aromatic ring count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_formal_charge",\\n      "description": "Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_qed",\\n      "description": "Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_heteroatoms",\\n      "description": "Return heteroatom count (non C/H).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "describe_high_level_fg_fragments",\\n      "description": "Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "The SMILES string to parse."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "remove_salts",\\n      "description": "Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "predict_pka",\\n      "description": "Predict pKa values for ionizable sites in a molecule. Returns base-site and acid-site pKa values (1-indexed atom map numbers), the most basic/acidic pKa, the number of basic/acidic sites, and the atom-mapped SMILES.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "estimate_logd",\\n      "description": "Estimate logD at a target pH from predicted pKa values and RDKit logP. Uses a Henderson-Hasselbalch approximation: logD(pH) ≈ logP + log10(f_neutral). logP is RDKit Wildman-Crippen logP. This is a heuristic intended for helpingyour reasoning and should not be treated as experimental logD. For polyprotic molecules, uses the most basic and most acidic predicted pKa values.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "ph": {\\n            "type": "number",\\n            "description": "Target pH (default: 7.4)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "score_structural_alerts",\\n      "description": "Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "alert_library": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "enum": [\\n              "all",\\n              "pains",\\n              "pains_a",\\n              "pains_b",\\n              "pains_c",\\n              "brenk",\\n              "nih",\\n              "zinc",\\n              "chembl",\\n              "chembl_bms",\\n              "chembl_lint",\\n              "chembl_mlsmr"\\n            ],\\n            "description": "Alert library to screen against (default: all)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_labute_asa",\\n      "description": "Return Labute approximate surface area (ASA proxy).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return fraction of aromatic atoms.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_heterocycles",\\n      "description": "Return heterocycle count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_aromatic_heterocycles",\\n      "description": "Return aromatic heterocycle count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_atom_stereo_centers",\\n      "description": "Return atom stereocenter count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_bertz_ct",\\n      "description": "Return Bertz complexity index.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_kappa1",\\n      "description": "Return Kappa1 shape index.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_kappa2",\\n      "description": "Return Kappa2 shape index.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n]\\nuser\\nInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: SARS-CoV-2 is a coronavirus that causes the COVID-19 disease and has led to millions of deaths worldwide within a few years. Discovering new drugs that can inhibit viral replication would be useful for combatting the virus. One method is to screen a library of small molecule drugs for inhibition in an infected cell-based assay.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) does not inhibit SARS-CoV-2 replication (B) inhibits SARS-CoV-2 replication\\nDrug SMILES: C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO\\n\\nPlease think step by step and use tools when necessary (**Don\'t use the same tool more than once**). Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"\\nassistant\\n<think>\\nThe user asks to predict whether a drug with given SMILES inhibits SARS-CoV-2 replication or not. We need to output either (A) does not inhibit or (B) inhibits. We have tools to compute molecular properties but not directly predict antiviral activity. However, we can use available tools to compute properties that might correlate with antiviral activity, such as molecular weight, logP, TPSA, etc. But there\'s no direct prediction tool. The user wants us to think step by step and use tools when necessary. We need to reason about the drug\'s properties and perhaps compare to known antiviral drugs. But we don\'t have a model for SARS-CoV-2 inhibition.\\n\\nWe can compute properties like molecular weight, logP, HBD, HBA, etc. Perhaps we can see if it matches Lipinski\'s rule of five for drug-likeness, but that doesn\'t guarantee antiviral activity. However, we might infer that molecules with certain properties (e.g., moderate molecular weight, reasonable logP, etc.) might be more likely to be bioactive. But this is speculative.\\n\\nAlternatively, we can compute structural alerts or known toxicophores that might indicate promiscuous binding or toxicity. But again, not directly.\\n\\nMaybe we can search for known drugs with similar structure? The SMILES looks like a steroid-like molecule (maybe cortisol or dexamethasone?). Let\'s examine: C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO. This appears to be a steroid with fluorine substitution, ketone, hydroxyl groups. Possibly a corticosteroid like dexamethasone or prednisolone. Dexamethasone is known to have anti-inflammatory effects but not directly antiviral against SARS-CoV-2. However, some corticosteroids have been used in COVID-19 treatment but not as direct antivirals.\\n\\nBut the question is about inhibiting SARS-CoV-2 replication, not anti-inflammatory. Dexamethasone does not inhibit viral replication directly; it suppresses immune response. So likely it does not inhibit SARS-CoV-2 replication.\\n\\nBut we need to base our prediction on computed properties. Let\'s compute some properties to see if it resembles known antiviral drugs. Let\'s use tools to get molecular weight, logP, TPSA, HBD, HBA, etc.\\n\\nFirst, get molecular weight.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_molecular_weight", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Average molecular weight (Daltons): 392.4670", "function_name": "get_molecular_weight", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nMolecular weight ~392.5 Da. That\'s within typical drug range (under 500). Now logP.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_mol_logp", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "cLogP estimate: 1.8957", "function_name": "get_mol_logp", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nlogP ~1.9, moderately lipophilic. TPSA.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_tpsa", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Topological Polar Surface Area (TPSA, \\u00c5^2): 94.8300", "function_name": "get_tpsa", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nTPSA ~94.8 Å^2, moderate polarity. HBD and HBA.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_hbd", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of H-bond donors: 3", "function_name": "get_hbd", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nHBD = 3. HBA.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_hba", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of H-bond acceptors: 5", "function_name": "get_hba", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nHBA = 5. Rotatable bonds.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of rotatable bonds: 2", "function_name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nRotatable bonds = 2, low flexibility. Heavy atom count.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_heavy_atom_count", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of heavy atoms: 28", "function_name": "get_heavy_atom_count", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nHeavy atoms 28. Ring count.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_ring_count", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Total ring count: 4", "function_name": "get_ring_count", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\n4 rings, likely steroid core. Aromatic rings? Let\'s check.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_num_aromatic_rings", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Aromatic ring count: 0", "function_name": "get_num_aromatic_rings", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nNo aromatic rings, all alicyclic. Fraction aromatic atoms.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Fraction aromatic atoms: 0.0000", "function_name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nFraction aromatic atoms 0. So it\'s a fully aliphatic steroid.\\n\\nNow, what about known SARS-CoV-2 inhibitors? Many are protease inhibitors (e.g., lopinavir, ritonavir) or RNA polymerase inhibitors (remdesivir, molnupiravir). They tend to be larger molecules with more heteroatoms, often contain aromatic rings. For example, remdesivir is a nucleoside analog with a complex structure. This steroid looks different.\\n\\nBut there are some steroids that have shown antiviral activity? Some glucocorticoids like dexamethasone have been used in COVID-19 but not as direct antivirals. However, there is some evidence that certain steroids can inhibit viral replication? I recall that some studies found that dexamethasone does not inhibit SARS-CoV-2 replication in vitro. In fact, corticosteroids may enhance viral replication by suppressing immune response. So likely not.\\n\\nThus, based on structural similarity to corticosteroids, which are not direct antivirals, I\'d predict (A) does not inhibit SARS-CoV-2 replication.\\n\\nBut we need to be careful: the question might be from a dataset where molecules are screened for antiviral activity. Perhaps we can compute QED (drug-likeness) and other properties. Let\'s compute QED.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_qed", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "QED (drug-likeness score, 0-1): 0.6672", "function_name": "get_qed", "arguments": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nQED ~0.67, moderate drug-likeness.\\n\\nNow, we could compute structural alerts to see if there are any toxicophores that might indicate promiscuous binding. Let\'s run structural alerts.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "score_structural_alerts", "parameters": {"smiles": "C[C@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@]4(C)[C@@]3(F)[C@@H](O)C[C@]2(C)[C@@]1(O)C(=O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "{\\n  \\"library\\": \\"all\\",\\n  \\"count\\": 5,\\n  \\"alerts\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\": \\"Ketone\\",\\n      \\"filter_set\\": \\"ChEMBL23_MLSMR\\",\\n      \\"scope\\": \\"ChEMBL 23 structural alerts rule-set: CHEMBL_MLSMR\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"description\\"