[CH2:8][NH:9] <-> [5*]CN[10*], \\n3. lactam:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: O.cc <-> [O:11].[cH:16][c:17] <-> [14*]cc([18*])[22*], \\n4. benzene:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: cccc.cn <-> [c:2][cH:3][cH:4][c:5].[c:22][n:23] <-> [1*]c([7*])ccc([6*])[8*], \\n5. pyridazine:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: O.Cnc(=O)c <-> [O:11].[CH2:12][n:13][c:14](=[O:15])[cH:16] <-> [10*]Cn([23*])c(=O)c[17*], \\n", "function_name": "describe_high_level_fg_fragments", "arguments": {"smiles": "COC1=CC=C(C=C1)CNC(=O)CN2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nThe molecule appears to have an aryl ether, a secondary amide, a lactam, a benzene ring, and a pyridazine-like ring (actually a fused ring system? Let\'s interpret the SMILES: COC1=CC=C(C=C1)CNC(=O)CN2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2. This looks like a compound with a methoxy phenyl group attached to a chain: C-NC(=O)-C-N-C(=O)-C=C-C-C-C-C-C-=N? Actually, the structure seems to have a benzene ring with methoxy, then a CH2-NH-C(=O)-CH2-N (with a carbonyl) attached to a fused ring system: N2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2. That\'s a fused bicyclic system: a pyridazine fused with a cyclohexane? Let\'s draw mentally: N2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2. This is a 1,2,4-triazine? Actually, it\'s a pyridazine with a carbonyl at position 2? The fragment is a lactam fused with a cyclohexane ring. Might be a fused ring system like a pyrrolidine? Wait, N2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2: the N2 is part of a ring? The SMILES indicates a ring closure: N2...=N2. So atom N2 is connected to another N via ring closure? Actually, the pattern "N2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2" suggests a bicyclic system where N2 is part of a 6-membered ring fused to a 6-membered ring? Let\'s parse: N2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2. The "3" is a ring closure label. So we have a ring: C=C3CCCCC3. That\'s a cyclohexene ring? Actually, C=C3CCCCC3 means a d