hould also consider the number of rotatable bonds (5) which is okay (typically <10).\\n\\nNow, let\'s get the Labute approximate surface area (ASA) as a proxy for size.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_labute_asa", "parameters": {"smiles": "COC1=CC=C(C=C1)CNC(=O)CN2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Labute approximate surface area (ASA proxy): 140.0064", "function_name": "get_labute_asa", "arguments": {"smiles": "COC1=CC=C(C=C1)CNC(=O)CN2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nFraction of aromatic atoms:\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms", "parameters": {"smiles": "COC1=CC=C(C=C1)CNC(=O)CN2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Fraction aromatic atoms: 0.5000", "function_name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms", "arguments": {"smiles": "COC1=CC=C(C=C1)CNC(=O)CN2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nNow, let\'s think about the molecule\'s charge. It has no formal charge, but amide and lactam could be partially charged at physiological pH? The pKa prediction might help. Let\'s get pKa values.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "predict_pka", "parameters": {"smiles": "COC1=CC=C(C=C1)CNC(=O)CN2C(=O)C=C3CCCCC3=N2"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Mapped SMILES: [C:1]([O:2][c:3]1[c:4]([H:28])[c:5]([H:29])[c:6]([C:7]([N:8]([C:9](=[O:10])[C:11]([n:12]2[n:13][c:14]3[c:15]([c:16]([H:35])[c:17]2=[O:18])[C:19]([H:36])([H:37])[C:20]([H:38])([H:39])[C:21]([H:40])([H:41])[C:22]3([H:42])[H:43])([H:33])[H:34])[H:32])([H:30])[H:31])[c:23]([H:44])[c:24]1[H:45])([H:25])([H:26])[H:27]\\nNumber of basic sites: 2\\nNumber of acidic sites: 1\\nBase-site pKa values (atom_map_number: pKa): {12: 3.1648306846618652, 13: 1.5282986164093018}\\nMost basic pKa: 3.1648\\nAcid-site pKa values (atom_map_number: pKa): {32: 13.792335510253906}\\nMost acidic pKa: 13.7923", "f