[36m(PPOTrainer pid=1890399)[0m INFO 02-25 17:39:03 ppo_trainer.py:410] [trace] step=75 reward=1.000 response_len=4162 total_len=7721 preview='system name=<|plugin|>\\nYou are an expert reasoner with extensive experience in all areas. You approach problems through systematic thinking and rigorous reasoning. Your response should reflect deep understanding and precise logical thinking, making your solution path and reasoning clear to others. Please put your thinking process within <think>...</think> tags.\\n\\nYour response should consist of a reasoning step (**thought**) followed immediately by a function call in valid JSON format. Wrap each function call using the `<|action_start|><|plugin|>` and `<|action_end|>` tags.\\n\\n**Format example:**\\n\\n```\\n(Your thought goes here...)\\n\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{\\n    "name": "tool_name",\\n    "parameters": {\\n        "parameter1": "value1",\\n        "parameter2": "value2"\\n    }\\n}\\n<|action_end|>\\n```\\n\\n# External Tools\\nYou have access to these tools:\\n[\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_molecular_weight",\\n      "description": "Return the average molecular weight (Daltons).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_exact_molecular_weight",\\n      "description": "Return the monoisotopic exact molecular weight (Daltons).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_heavy_atom_count",\\n      "description": "Return the number of non-hydrogen atoms (heavy atoms).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_mol_logp",\\n      "description": "Return Wildman–Crippen cLogP (octanol/water).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_tpsa",\\n      "description": "Return topological polar surface area TPSA (Å^2).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_hbd",\\n      "description": "Return Lipinski H-bond donors count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_hba",\\n      "description": "Return Lipinski H-bond acceptors count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds",\\n      "description": "Return rotatable bonds count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_fraction_csp3",\\n      "description": "Return FractionCSP3 = (# sp3 carbon atoms)/(# total carbon atoms), Often used as a simple “3D character / saturation” proxy from 2D structure.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_mol_mr",\\n      "description": "Return Wildman–Crippen molar refractivity (MR).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_ring_count",\\n      "description": "Return total ring count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_aromatic_rings",\\n      "description": "Return aromatic ring count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_formal_charge",\\n      "description": "Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_qed",\\n      "description": "Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_heteroatoms",\\n      "description": "Return heteroatom count (non C/H).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "describe_high_level_fg_fragments",\\n      "description": "Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "The SMILES string to parse."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "remove_salts",\\n      "description": "Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "predict_pka",\\n      "description": "Predict pKa values for ionizable sites in a molecule. Returns base-site and acid-site pKa values (1-indexed atom map numbers), the most basic/acidic pKa, the number of basic/acidic sites, and the atom-mapped SMILES.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "estimate_logd",\\n      "description": "Estimate logD at a target pH from predicted pKa values and RDKit logP. Uses a Henderson-Hasselbalch approximation: logD(pH) ≈ logP + log10(f_neutral). logP is RDKit Wildman-Crippen logP. This is a heuristic intended for helpingyour reasoning and should not be treated as experimental logD. For polyprotic molecules, uses the most basic and most acidic predicted pKa values.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "ph": {\\n            "type": "number",\\n            "description": "Target pH (default: 7.4)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "score_structural_alerts",\\n      "description": "Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "alert_library": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "enum": [\\n              "all",\\n              "pains",\\n              "pains_a",\\n              "pains_b",\\n              "pains_c",\\n              "brenk",\\n              "nih",\\n              "zinc",\\n              "chembl",\\n              "chembl_bms",\\n              "chembl_lint",\\n              "chembl_mlsmr"\\n            ],\\n            "description": "Alert library to screen against (default: all)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_labute_asa",\\n      "description": "Return Labute approximate surface area (ASA proxy) using RDKit rdMolDescriptors.CalcLabuteASA.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return fraction of aromatic atoms (aromatic atoms / total atoms).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_positive_charge_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return count of cationic centers (atoms with formal charge > 0).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_negative_charge_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return count of anionic centers (atoms with formal charge < 0).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n]\\nuser\\nInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: Oral bioavailability is defined as “the rate and extent to which the active ingredient or active moiety is absorbed from a drug product and becomes available at the site of action”.\\n\\n\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) has oral bioavailability < 20% (B) has oral bioavailability ≥ 20%\\nDrug SMILES: OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO\\n\\nPlease think step by step and use tools when necessary (**Don\'t use the same tool more than once**). Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"\\nassistant\\n<think>\\nThe user asks to predict oral bioavailability (<20% or ≥20%) for a given drug SMILES: OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO. This is a sugar molecule, likely a hexose like glucose or similar. Let\'s examine: OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO. This is a linear representation of a sugar with multiple hydroxyl groups. It\'s likely D-glucose or similar. Let\'s parse: O-C-C-C(O)-C(O)-C(O)-C(O)-C-O. Actually, it\'s a chain of 6 carbons, each with hydroxyl groups (except ends). This is a typical sugar alcohol like sorbitol or similar. Actually, sorbitol is a sugar alcohol with 6 carbons, each with hydroxyl groups. The SMILES: OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO. This is a linear chain: C1-OH, C2-OH, C3-OH, C4-OH, C5-OH, C6-OH. That\'s a hexitol. Sorbitol is a sugar alcohol used as sweetener. Oral bioavailability of sorbitol? Sorbitol is poorly absorbed in the small intestine, but can be absorbed via active transport. However, the question is about oral bioavailability <20% or ≥20%. I need to think about typical drug-like properties.\\n\\nWe need to predict oral bioavailability based on molecular properties. There are known rules like Lipinski\'s Rule of Five, Veber\'s rules, etc. For oral bioavailability, key factors include molecular weight, logP, hydrogen bond donors/acceptors, polar surface area, rotatable bonds, etc.\\n\\nLet\'s compute some properties using the tools. We\'ll get molecular weight, logP, TPSA, HBD, HBA, rotatable bonds, etc. Then we can assess likelihood of good oral bioavailability.\\n\\nFirst, let\'s get molecular weight. Use get_molecular_weight.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_molecular_weight", "parameters": {"smiles": "OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Average molecular weight (Daltons): 182.1720", "function_name": "get_molecular_weight", "arguments": {"smiles": "OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nAverage molecular weight 182.17 Da. That\'s well below 500 Da (Lipinski limit). Good.\\n\\nNow get logP: use get_mol_logp.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_mol_logp", "parameters": {"smiles": "OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "cLogP estimate: -3.5854", "function_name": "get_mol_logp", "arguments": {"smiles": "OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\ncLogP is -3.585, very hydrophilic. Lipinski rule says logP should be <5 (or <4.15). This is fine.\\n\\nTPSA: get_tpsa.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_tpsa", "parameters": {"smiles": "OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Topological Polar Surface Area (TPSA, \\u00c5^2): 121.3800", "function_name": "get_tpsa", "arguments": {"smiles": "OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nTPSA is 121.38 Å². Veber\'s rule suggests TPSA <140 Å² and rotatable bonds ≤10 for good oral bioavailability. This is fine.\\n\\nHBD: get_hbd.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_hbd", "parameters": {"smiles": "OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of H-bond donors: 6", "function_name": "get_hbd", "arguments": {"smiles": "OC[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nHBD count is 6. Lipinski rule says HBD ≤5. This exceeds by 1. However, Lipinski\'s rule is for drug-likeness, not strict bioavailability. But many drugs with HBD >5 can still be orally bioavailable.\\n\\nHBA: get_hba.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_hba", "param