[36m(PPOTrainer pid=1890399)[0m INFO 02-25 19:57:59 ppo_trainer.py:410] [trace] step=95 reward=1.000 response_len=8550 total_len=12194 preview='system name=<|plugin|>\\nYou are an expert reasoner with extensive experience in all areas. You approach problems through systematic thinking and rigorous reasoning. Your response should reflect deep understanding and precise logical thinking, making your solution path and reasoning clear to others. Please put your thinking process within <think>...</think> tags.\\n\\nYour response should consist of a reasoning step (**thought**) followed immediately by a function call in valid JSON format. Wrap each function call using the `<|action_start|><|plugin|>` and `<|action_end|>` tags.\\n\\n**Format example:**\\n\\n```\\n(Your thought goes here...)\\n\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{\\n    "name": "tool_name",\\n    "parameters": {\\n        "parameter1": "value1",\\n        "parameter2": "value2"\\n    }\\n}\\n<|action_end|>\\n```\\n\\n# External Tools\\nYou have access to these tools:\\n[\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_molecular_weight",\\n      "description": "Return the average molecular weight (Daltons).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_exact_molecular_weight",\\n      "description": "Return the monoisotopic exact molecular weight (Daltons).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_heavy_atom_count",\\n      "description": "Return the number of non-hydrogen atoms (heavy atoms).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_mol_logp",\\n      "description": "Return Wildman–Crippen cLogP (octanol/water).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_tpsa",\\n      "description": "Return topological polar surface area TPSA (Å^2).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_hbd",\\n      "description": "Return Lipinski H-bond donors count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_hba",\\n      "description": "Return Lipinski H-bond acceptors count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds",\\n      "description": "Return rotatable bonds count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_fraction_csp3",\\n      "description": "Return FractionCSP3 = (# sp3 carbon atoms)/(# total carbon atoms), Often used as a simple “3D character / saturation” proxy from 2D structure.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_mol_mr",\\n      "description": "Return Wildman–Crippen molar refractivity (MR).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_ring_count",\\n      "description": "Return total ring count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_aromatic_rings",\\n      "description": "Return aromatic ring count.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_formal_charge",\\n      "description": "Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_qed",\\n      "description": "Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_heteroatoms",\\n      "description": "Return heteroatom count (non C/H).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "describe_high_level_fg_fragments",\\n      "description": "Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "The SMILES string to parse."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "remove_salts",\\n      "description": "Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "predict_pka",\\n      "description": "Predict pKa values for ionizable sites in a molecule. Returns base-site and acid-site pKa values (1-indexed atom map numbers), the most basic/acidic pKa, the number of basic/acidic sites, and the atom-mapped SMILES.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "estimate_logd",\\n      "description": "Estimate logD at a target pH from predicted pKa values and RDKit logP. Uses a Henderson-Hasselbalch approximation: logD(pH) ≈ logP + log10(f_neutral). logP is RDKit Wildman-Crippen logP. This is a heuristic intended for helpingyour reasoning and should not be treated as experimental logD. For polyprotic molecules, uses the most basic and most acidic predicted pKa values.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "ph": {\\n            "type": "number",\\n            "description": "Target pH (default: 7.4)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "score_structural_alerts",\\n      "description": "Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          },\\n          "alert_library": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "enum": [\\n              "all",\\n              "pains",\\n              "pains_a",\\n              "pains_b",\\n              "pains_c",\\n              "brenk",\\n              "nih",\\n              "zinc",\\n              "chembl",\\n              "chembl_bms",\\n              "chembl_lint",\\n              "chembl_mlsmr"\\n            ],\\n            "description": "Alert library to screen against (default: all)."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_labute_asa",\\n      "description": "Return Labute approximate surface area (ASA proxy) using RDKit rdMolDescriptors.CalcLabuteASA.",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_fraction_aromatic_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return fraction of aromatic atoms (aromatic atoms / total atoms).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_positive_charge_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return count of cationic centers (atoms with formal charge > 0).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  },\\n  {\\n    "type": "function",\\n    "function": {\\n      "name": "get_num_negative_charge_atoms",\\n      "description": "Return count of anionic centers (atoms with formal charge < 0).",\\n      "parameters": {\\n        "type": "object",\\n        "properties": {\\n          "smiles": {\\n            "type": "string",\\n            "description": "SMILES string of the molecule."\\n          }\\n        },\\n        "required": [\\n          "smiles"\\n        ],\\n        "additionalProperties": false\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n]\\nuser\\nInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: Membrane permeability is a measure of the ability of a drug to pass through a cellular membrane through passive diffusion, which is necessary for the drug to affect a cell. In general, small hydrophobic drugs can easily pass through membranes whereas large polar/charged drugs do not easily pass through. PAMPA (parallel artificial membrane permeability assay) is a commonly employed non-cell-based assay to evaluate drug permeability across the cellular membrane.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) is not permeable to cell membranes in a PAMPA assay (B) is permeable to cell membranes in a PAMPA assay\\nDrug SMILES: CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]\\n\\nPlease think step by step and use tools when necessary (**Don\'t use the same tool more than once**). Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"\\nassistant\\n<think>\\nI need to analyze this drug\'s SMILES to predict its membrane permeability. The SMILES is: CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]\\n\\nFirst, let me understand the structure. It looks complex with multiple functional groups. I should calculate various properties that relate to permeability: molecular weight, logP, polar surface area, hydrogen bond donors/acceptors, rotatable bonds, etc.\\n\\nLet me start by getting basic properties. First, molecular weight.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_molecular_weight", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Average molecular weight (Daltons): 464.5470", "function_name": "get_molecular_weight", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nMolecular weight is 464.5 Da, which is relatively high. Typically, drugs with MW >500 have reduced permeability, but 464 is borderline.\\n\\nNow, logP (hydrophobicity). Let\'s get clogP.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_mol_logp", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "cLogP estimate: 4.5364", "function_name": "get_mol_logp", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nclogP is 4.54, which is quite lipophilic. That favors membrane permeability.\\n\\nTopological polar surface area (TPSA) - important for permeability. Let\'s get that.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_tpsa", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Topological Polar Surface Area (TPSA, \\u00c5^2): 137.9100", "function_name": "get_tpsa", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nTPSA is 137.9 Å². Typically, drugs with TPSA >140 Å² have poor permeability. 137.9 is borderline but maybe okay.\\n\\nHydrogen bond donors and acceptors. Let\'s get those.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_hbd", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of H-bond donors: 3", "function_name": "get_hbd", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nHBD = 3. That\'s moderate. Lipinski\'s rule says ≤5 is okay. HBA?\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_hba", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of H-bond acceptors: 5", "function_name": "get_hba", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nHBA = 5. That\'s moderate.\\n\\nRotatable bonds count: flexibility affects permeability. Let\'s get that.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Number of rotatable bonds: 7", "function_name": "get_num_rotatable_bonds", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\n7 rotatable bonds is moderate.\\n\\nNow, the molecule has a sulfoxide group? Actually, it\'s a sulfonamide with a positive sulfur and negative oxygen? The SMILES shows [S+](=O)(...)[O-] which is a sulfonate group (sulfonic acid derivative). That\'s highly polar and charged. Also there\'s a nitrile group (C#N). There\'s also an amide group NC(=O). So overall, the molecule has both hydrophobic and polar/charged groups.\\n\\nLet\'s examine formal charge. The SMILES includes [S+] and [O-], so it\'s a zwitterion? Actually, it\'s a sulfonate group where sulfur is positively charged and oxygen negatively charged. That\'s a strong ionic character. Also there might be other charges? Let\'s get formal charge.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "get_formal_charge", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Net formal charge: 0", "function_name": "get_formal_charge", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nNet formal charge is 0, but there is a zwitterionic pair (S+ and O-). That could still affect permeability because charged species don\'t passively diffuse well. However, at physiological pH (7.4), the molecule might exist in neutral form if the sulfonate is deprotonated? Wait, sulfonate is SO3- usually, but here it\'s [S+](=O)(...)[O-] which is a sulfonamide anion? Actually, sulfonamide is R-SO2-NR2, but here it\'s S+ with two oxygens double-bonded and one oxygen single-bonded with negative charge. That\'s a sulfonate group (R-SO3-). But the SMILES shows [S+](=O)(...)[O-] meaning sulfur has positive charge and one oxygen has negative charge. That\'s a sulfonate anion with a counterion? The net charge is zero, so maybe there\'s a cation elsewhere? Let\'s check other parts: there\'s an amine group? The structure has NC(=O) which is an amide nitrogen, not basic. There\'s also a tertiary amine in the pyrrole ring? The N2 is part of a pyrrole ring? Actually, the structure includes a pyrrole ring with N2, C(=O)C, etc. That nitrogen is likely not basic because it\'s in an aromatic ring. So the only charged group is the sulfonate anion, but the net charge is zero, meaning there is a cation somewhere. Maybe the sulfur is positively charged? That would be a sulfonium ion? Unlikely. Let\'s examine the SMILES more carefully.\\n\\nSMILES: CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]\\n\\nBreakdown:\\n- CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C) : This looks like a biphenyl-like structure with an amide linkage to a pyrrole ring.\\n- [S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-] : This is a sulfonate group attached to the pyrrole ring? Actually, the sulfonate is attached via the sulfur to the pyrrole ring? The SMILES shows ...C)[S+](=O)(...)[O-] meaning the sulfur is bonded to the pyrrole ring (the C before S+). So it\'s a sulfonate ester? Wait, sulfonate is SO3-, but here sulfur is bonded to three oxygens: two double-bonded oxygens, one oxygen with negative charge, and also bonded to the pyrrole carbon? That would be a sulfonate group where sulfur is bonded to three oxygens and one carbon (making it a sulfonate ester). Actually, typical sulfonate is R-SO3-, where sulfur is bonded to three oxygens and one carbon (R). Here, the sulfur is positively charged, which is unusual. Maybe it\'s a sulfonamide anion where the nitrogen is part of the aromatic ring? The NC3... suggests a nitrogen attached to the sulfonate oxygen? Actually, the sulfonate is attached to a nitrogen: [S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]. So sulfur is bonded to an oxygen double-bonded, another oxygen double-bonded, a nitrogen (from aniline ring), and an oxygen with negative charge. That\'s a sulfonamide anion where the sulfur has a positive charge? That seems odd. Maybe it\'s a sulfonamide with a deprotonated nitrogen? The nitrogen is part of an aniline ring, so it\'s aromatic and not basic. The sulfonate group might be neutral overall.\\n\\nBetter to get a description of functional groups. Let\'s use describe_high_level_fg_fragments.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "describe_high_level_fg_fragments", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Original SMILES: CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]\\nwith atom ids marked: C[CH2:1][c:2]1[c:3]([S+:21](=[O:22])([NH:23][c:24]2[cH:25][cH:26][cH:27][c:28]([C:30]#[N:31])[cH:29]2)[O-:32])[cH:4][c:5]([NH:8][C:9](=[O:10])[c:11]2[c:12]([CH3:20])[c:13]([C:17](=[O:18])[CH3:19])[c:14]([CH3:16])[nH:15]2)[cH:6][cH:7]1.\\nThe functional groups inside the molecule are:\\n1. amine:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: c <-> [c:24] <-> [23*]c([25*])[29*], \\n2. ketone:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: cC <-> [c:14][CH3:16] <-> [13*]c([15*])C, \\n3. secondary amide:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: cc.N <-> [cH:6][cH:7].[NH:8] <-> [2*]cc[5*], \\n4. nitrile:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: C#N <-> [C:30]#[N:31] <-> [28*]C#N, \\n5. sulfanylidene:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: [S+] <-> [S+:21] <-> [3*][S+](=[22*])([23*])[32*], \\n6. benzene:\\n   Count:2\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: cccc.C.C <-> [c:2][c:3][cH:4][c:5].[CH3:19].[CH3:20] <-> [1*]c([7*])c([21*])cc([6*])[8*],  ccccc.[O-] <-> [cH:25][cH:26][cH:27][c:28][cH:29].[O-:32] <-> [24*]cccc([30*])c[24*], \\n7. 1H-pyrrole:\\n   Count:1\\n   Corresponding fragment SMILES <-> with atom ids <-> with attachment points: C(=O)c.cC <-> [C:9](=[O:10])[c:11].[c:13][C:17] <-> [8*]C(=O)c([12*])[15*], \\n", "function_name": "describe_high_level_fg_fragments", "arguments": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\nassistant\\n\\n<think>\\nThe description shows a sulfanylidene group (S+) with attachment points. That\'s a sulfonium ion? Actually, sulfanylidene is S=, but here it\'s [S+] meaning sulfur with positive charge. That\'s unusual. The molecule also has a nitrile, amide, ketone, aromatic rings.\\n\\nNow, permeability prediction: Generally, passive diffusion requires lipophilicity, low polar surface area, low hydrogen bonding, and neutral charge. This molecule has high lipophilicity (clogP 4.54), moderate TPSA (137.9), moderate HBD/HBA, but has a charged sulfonate group. However, the net charge is zero, so maybe at pH 7.4, the molecule is neutral. But sulfonate anions are highly polar and usually not permeable. However, if the sulfonate is part of a zwitterion, the overall molecule might be neutral but still have high polarity.\\n\\nLet\'s check the pKa to see if there are ionizable groups. Use predict_pka.\\n</think>\\n<|action_start|><|plugin|>\\n{"name": "predict_pka", "parameters": {"smiles": "CCC1=C(C=C(C=C1)NC(=O)C2=C(C(=C(N2)C)C(=O)C)C)[S+](=O)(NC3=CC=CC(=C3)C#N)[O-]"}}\\n<|action_end|>\\nenvironment name=<|plugin|>\\n\\n{"result": "Mapped SMILES: [C:1]([C:2]([c:3]1[c:4]([H:39])[c:5]([H:40])[c:6]([N:7]([C:8](=[O:9])[c:10]2[n